WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.166
Everything in this harbour is made up,
except the kind of room in that window.

2
00:00:11.616 --> 00:00:16.909
It is a chat room, the very example
Durable Objects began with, in 2020.

3
00:00:17.459 --> 00:00:21.522
Someone types message
forty-two: the ferry is late.

4
00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:27.799
It waits at the door. Then it gets
its tick: a promise that it is saved.

5
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.874
Then House A goes dark,
and the square goes quiet.

6
00:00:37.724 --> 00:00:42.321
Up the hill, House C lights.
Its keeper, the runtime,

7
00:00:42.321 --> 00:00:46.558
draws a bundle from the well, and
the room relights in a new window.

8
00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:49.852
Message forty-two is still
there, with its tick.

9
00:00:50.602 --> 00:00:55.645
The machine that made that promise
is gone. The promise is still true.

10
00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:00.443
How did the promise outlive the machine?

11
00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:05.701
The answer is in the square: the
town well, which is a storage bucket.

12
00:01:06.001 --> 00:01:08.589
The plate above it names the room's owner.

13
00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:12.673
The room keeps two promises:
one owner at a time,

14
00:01:12.673 --> 00:01:15.876
and nothing acknowledged is
lost when a machine fails.

15
00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:21.446
And a newer one, still being built: the
room can move between machines you run.

16
00:01:21.996 --> 00:01:27.324
So how can a bucket, which cannot run a
single line of code, make a room portable?

17
00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:36.284
A story from 2020 to the end of October
2026: a room, a well and a ribbon.

18
00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:40.252
The harbour is ours. The
machinery is real code,

19
00:01:40.252 --> 00:01:42.886
and every piece wears a tag saying whose.

20
00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:46.417
The newest of it is days
old, and still experimental.

21
00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:53.094
On the twenty-eighth of September, 2020,

22
00:01:53.094 --> 00:01:58.178
Kenton Varda of Cloudflare introduced
Durable Objects, in a closed beta.

23
00:01:58.628 --> 00:02:02.679
Each object has an identifier that
is unique across the whole world.

24
00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:06.415
And his post is plain about
where that object lives.

25
00:02:07.165 --> 00:02:10.520
In one place. Only ever one, at a time.

26
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.068
So every request about the same
thing reaches the same place,

27
00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:16.913
and that place can coordinate them.

28
00:02:18.392 --> 00:02:23.001
His demonstration is a chat app:
each chat room is one object,

29
00:02:23.001 --> 00:02:25.996
and every message passes
through it to everyone else.

30
00:02:26.296 --> 00:02:31.938
That is the kind of room our lantern
holds: one room, one object, one home.

31
00:02:32.588 --> 00:02:37.093
Notice what one home does not
mean. A handler can stop and wait,

32
00:02:37.093 --> 00:02:41.411
and living in one place does not
make all its steps one transaction.

33
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:51.105
The third of August, 2021. A later post of his
is called Easy, Fast, Correct: Choose three.

34
00:02:51.555 --> 00:02:55.235
It puts a gate on the
room's door, an output gate.

35
00:02:55.235 --> 00:02:59.646
While a write is unfinished, the reply
to our sender waits behind a latch.

36
00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:03.870
The write completes. The latch
lifts, and the letter goes out.

37
00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.961
If the write fails, the waiting
letters are thrown away,

38
00:03:07.961 --> 00:03:11.106
replaced with errors,
and the object restarts.

39
00:03:11.406 --> 00:03:15.068
So the tick in the harbour
was a held letter, released.

40
00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:22.949
September 2022. Cloudflare opens
the source of its runtime, workerd.

41
00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:27.114
Now the runtime could leave
Cloudflare, and a room could come too,

42
00:03:27.114 --> 00:03:30.179
but only as a single
instance on one machine.

43
00:03:30.179 --> 00:03:36.309
In 2026 Ryan Dahl would call it exactly
that: limited to a single instance.

44
00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:44.388
September 2024. SQLite moves inside the
object, into the very same thread as its code.

45
00:03:44.688 --> 00:03:47.114
Local reads become almost instant.

46
00:03:47.564 --> 00:03:53.032
But a tick is not a local read. A tick
says the write will outlive the machine.

47
00:03:54.683 --> 00:04:01.311
On Cloudflare's own network, a hosted service
kept that promise: the Storage Relay Service.

48
00:04:01.761 --> 00:04:07.044
It watches SQLite's write-ahead log,
where each change is written first,

49
00:04:07.044 --> 00:04:10.701
and ships the changes to
object storage in batches:

50
00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:14.009
ten seconds, or sixteen
megabytes, at the most.

51
00:04:14.309 --> 00:04:18.674
Object storage is slow, and nobody
can wait ten seconds for a tick.

52
00:04:19.124 --> 00:04:22.711
So each commit also goes
straight to five followers,

53
00:04:22.711 --> 00:04:28.026
in five different data centres.
When three answer, the latch lifts.

54
00:04:28.326 --> 00:04:32.238
Real machinery. And all of
it lived inside Cloudflare.

55
00:04:36.125 --> 00:04:39.829
Back to the square. In our
harbour the Well stands

56
00:04:39.829 --> 00:04:43.647
for a bucket in an object
store, like Amazon's S3.

57
00:04:43.997 --> 00:04:47.782
There was a time when
overwriting a file in S3 and

58
00:04:47.782 --> 00:04:50.916
reading it straight back
could hand you the old copy.

59
00:04:51.216 --> 00:04:54.641
Amazon later explained
why: a write could take

60
00:04:54.641 --> 00:04:57.775
one path through its
caches, and a read another.

61
00:04:58.225 --> 00:05:04.784
On the first of December, 2020, that changed:
read after a write, and you get the latest.

62
00:05:05.084 --> 00:05:07.742
Whatever bucket you draw up is the newest.

63
00:05:09.433 --> 00:05:14.111
The job: the room's Plate is empty,
and Houses A and B both want it.

64
00:05:14.461 --> 00:05:16.296
Fresh reads are not enough.

65
00:05:16.296 --> 00:05:20.196
Pull the pin from the Well's brass
tooth, and the Well checks nothing.

66
00:05:20.746 --> 00:05:25.111
Each keeper reads the Plate:
nobody. Each writes: mine.

67
00:05:25.461 --> 00:05:28.769
Both lanterns light;
both rooms take messages.

68
00:05:29.069 --> 00:05:35.465
B's plate lands a moment later and wins the
Well. But A has already answered its senders.

69
00:05:35.915 --> 00:05:40.733
An overwrite can win the well. It cannot
take back what was already served.

70
00:05:41.383 --> 00:05:45.515
Between the read and the write,
the world moved. Pin back.

71
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.897
The twentieth of August, 2024.

72
00:05:51.897 --> 00:05:57.086
S3 learns its first refusal: write
this only if nothing is there.

73
00:05:57.436 --> 00:06:03.670
Two keepers race. One creation lands, a
shutter drops, and the second card is refused.

74
00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:10.249
The twenty-fifth of November, 2024: replace
this only if it is still the version I read.

75
00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:13.560
Both cards carry the same old version.

76
00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.234
One click: accepted, and the Plate's
notch is cut anew. One thunk: refused.

77
00:06:20.684 --> 00:06:25.570
That is compare and swap, and the store
checks the condition, not the keeper.

78
00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.121
The Well had learned to say no.

79
00:06:30.383 --> 00:06:32.507
So who referees the referee?

80
00:06:33.257 --> 00:06:38.783
In April 2021, Werner Vogels,
Amazon's chief technology officer,

81
00:06:38.783 --> 00:06:41.685
described what they had built inside S3.

82
00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:46.203
Caches that agree with one another,
an order for every object's changes,

83
00:06:46.203 --> 00:06:52.482
and a witness, told of every change,
that any cache can ask: is my copy stale?

84
00:06:52.932 --> 00:06:55.567
They checked it with
proofs and model checking,

85
00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:58.283
which, he wrote, was more
work than building it.

86
00:06:58.833 --> 00:07:03.047
So the houses on the hill run
no consensus of their own.

87
00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:05.833
The coordination has
moved inside the Well.

88
00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.605
Now a warning, from
celld's own documentation.

89
00:07:11.855 --> 00:07:16.604
Some stores speak S3's language
but do not keep these conditions;

90
00:07:16.604 --> 00:07:19.226
on them, two houses can own one room.

91
00:07:19.576 --> 00:07:23.441
Worse, a store can accept the
condition and silently ignore it.

92
00:07:23.891 --> 00:07:29.359
So before it serves, every celld
node makes four test writes: create,

93
00:07:29.359 --> 00:07:35.605
refuse a second create, update,
refuse a stale update. Two must fail.

94
00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:40.440
Then it checks a ranged read.
No node can skip the test.

95
00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.470
A bucket earns its place
by refusing, on demand.

96
00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:52.514
One more thing to notice. The Well referees
one key at a time: one Plate for one room.

97
00:07:52.864 --> 00:07:56.067
It makes no promise that
two rooms commit as one.

98
00:07:56.517 --> 00:08:00.974
And if the Well goes quiet, no
house can take a Plate at all.

99
00:08:03.833 --> 00:08:10.113
August, 2026. A second desk, a second
lamp: the Deno team releases celld.

100
00:08:10.563 --> 00:08:14.927
Ryan Dahl, of Deno, had started
it, and he later told us why.

101
00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:21.426
He wanted a single binary, written in Rust,
whose only outside service is object storage.

102
00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:27.611
celld runs a Workers application on
machines you own: Durable Objects,

103
00:08:27.611 --> 00:08:31.546
self-hosted and distributed,
with long-term state in a bucket.

104
00:08:31.846 --> 00:08:35.352
Its README says it needs
no consensus service.

105
00:08:35.352 --> 00:08:39.426
By now we know where the
consensus went: inside the well.

106
00:08:39.876 --> 00:08:44.333
Its log replicator descends from a
Rust reimplementation of Litestream.

107
00:08:45.808 --> 00:08:49.256
In October the two would
write one post together.

108
00:08:49.256 --> 00:08:52.727
In it, Kenton Varda, of
Cloudflare, named the gap.

109
00:08:53.027 --> 00:08:59.111
workerd's Durable Objects ran as a single
instance: good enough for local testing,

110
00:08:59.111 --> 00:09:04.717
unable to scale. He called it workerd's
one big gap in production readiness.

111
00:09:05.167 --> 00:09:10.414
And he wrote that when the Deno team released
celld in August, “we were delighted.”

112
00:09:10.964 --> 00:09:15.038
Two lamps in one frame. Two
ropes, beginning to braid.

113
00:09:16.792 --> 00:09:20.159
On the ninth of October,
2026, Deno and Cloudflare

114
00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:24.187
announced that the whole Deno
team was joining Cloudflare.

115
00:09:24.637 --> 00:09:26.703
These are plans, as announced.

116
00:09:26.703 --> 00:09:31.114
The Deno runtime gets one more year
of bug-fix and security releases.

117
00:09:31.414 --> 00:09:37.764
After that year, Ryan Dahl writes, the team's
work on the Deno runtime comes to an end.

118
00:09:38.214 --> 00:09:46.607
Deno Deploy runs six more months. JSR and
rusty_v8 continue. Deno stays open source.

119
00:09:46.907 --> 00:09:49.949
Cloudflare filed its
post under acquisitions,

120
00:09:49.949 --> 00:09:55.010
yet neither post states a price or
any terms. We will not guess at them.

121
00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.031
Ryan Dahl and Bert Belder
are to lead a first-class,

122
00:09:59.031 --> 00:10:01.887
supported way to run workerd yourself.

123
00:10:05.758 --> 00:10:09.462
On the evening of the twenty-eighth
of October, Pacific time,

124
00:10:09.462 --> 00:10:13.884
Ryan Dahl's commit landed in
workerd, calling itself a beginning.

125
00:10:14.334 --> 00:10:19.570
Several processes share one bucket;
each object has one owner at a time,

126
00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:23.169
and if an owner dies, the message
says, another picks it up.

127
00:10:23.469 --> 00:10:25.733
It is experimental. For now,

128
00:10:25.733 --> 00:10:29.331
a write is acknowledged only
once the bucket holds all of it.

129
00:10:29.681 --> 00:10:33.256
And one plain line: “Alarms
are not supported yet.”

130
00:10:33.806 --> 00:10:38.101
Two days later, alarms
landed. The next day, facets.

131
00:10:38.551 --> 00:10:39.618
Let us open it.

132
00:10:41.675 --> 00:10:46.029
Open the Plate. In workerd's
cluster it has three tiles:

133
00:10:46.029 --> 00:10:50.056
the house that owns the room, an
epoch, and a third, called from.

134
00:10:50.406 --> 00:10:54.202
The epoch is a count that
goes up with every new claim.

135
00:10:54.202 --> 00:10:57.766
The from tile names the last
epoch that actually opened.

136
00:10:58.116 --> 00:11:02.225
The code calls it the only
authority on who owns the room.

137
00:11:02.675 --> 00:11:08.004
To claim, a keeper offers the version it
read. If the Plate names a house whose

138
00:11:08.004 --> 00:11:13.715
lease has run out, the claim writes the
next epoch, and at most one claimant wins.

139
00:11:14.065 --> 00:11:18.384
If that house is still alive,
the request goes there instead.

140
00:11:18.384 --> 00:11:21.111
A brand-new room is born already open.

141
00:11:23.175 --> 00:11:29.072
A house may serve only while it holds a lease:
a candle on its sill, relit at each renewal.

142
00:11:29.422 --> 00:11:34.728
In workerd the lease is twenty
seconds. A house renews every three,

143
00:11:34.728 --> 00:11:38.523
gives each attempt four, and
retries a failure after one.

144
00:11:38.873 --> 00:11:41.497
A clock may be two
seconds off the bucket's,

145
00:11:41.497 --> 00:11:46.302
so the painted band at the candle's
foot is twice that: four seconds.

146
00:11:46.752 --> 00:11:50.270
If this lease runs out at
twenty thousand milliseconds,

147
00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:54.611
the house may serve only while its
clock reads under sixteen thousand.

148
00:11:54.961 --> 00:11:58.491
And it may start a renewal
only under twelve thousand,

149
00:11:58.491 --> 00:12:02.356
so even a full four-second
attempt ends before the band.

150
00:12:02.806 --> 00:12:05.974
Lines on a ruler, not
promises about timers.

151
00:12:08.433 --> 00:12:13.668
Why give each renewal four seconds,
and not two? The code keeps a scar.

152
00:12:14.018 --> 00:12:17.931
A comment records that
Cloudflare's R2 stalls writes for

153
00:12:17.931 --> 00:12:21.402
about five and a half seconds
at every five-minute mark,

154
00:12:21.402 --> 00:12:24.617
and that a two-second limit
once stopped every node.

155
00:12:25.067 --> 00:12:30.162
So failed renewals are retried while the
lease still holds through the attempt.

156
00:12:32.017 --> 00:12:35.360
A's keeper falls asleep: a paused process.

157
00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.995
Pull the pin, so A never
checks its own candle.

158
00:12:39.545 --> 00:12:45.361
The candle burns through the band. B sees that
A's lease has run out, and claims the room.

159
00:12:45.711 --> 00:12:48.682
A wakes and goes on serving. Two rooms.

160
00:12:49.332 --> 00:12:53.779
Pin back. Waking, A
checks its candle first.

161
00:12:53.779 --> 00:12:59.223
Past the band, it serves nothing, closes
its own shutters, and its lamp goes out.

162
00:12:59.573 --> 00:13:04.507
A house that cannot renew in time does
the same while its lease still holds:

163
00:13:04.507 --> 00:13:08.244
gone before anyone may
take over. That is fencing.

164
00:13:08.894 --> 00:13:14.849
Notice what B never knew. A timeout
proves silence. It does not prove death.

165
00:13:15.299 --> 00:13:19.931
Safety comes from A stopping itself,
and from a second look at the Plate.

166
00:13:22.017 --> 00:13:26.057
One soft spot: each house measures
its clock against the bucket's,

167
00:13:26.057 --> 00:13:28.297
and when it cannot, it assumes zero.

168
00:13:28.647 --> 00:13:33.239
So the clock is not the last line
of defence; the second look is.

169
00:13:33.689 --> 00:13:38.020
celld, a different implementation,
uses ten-second leases,

170
00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:43.429
and when the Well proves a write it does
the same: it reads the record, not a clock.

171
00:13:46.092 --> 00:13:50.840
Claimed is not opened, and the difference
decides which past the room comes back with.

172
00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:58.088
Made-up numbers. A opened epoch seven:
A, seven, from seven. The crown is lit.

173
00:13:58.438 --> 00:14:04.985
B claims: B, eight, from seven. The from
tile stays at seven, and the crown is dark.

174
00:14:05.335 --> 00:14:09.910
To open, B must restore
seven's log, upload a snapshot,

175
00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.670
and then make the opening write
that sets the from tile to eight.

176
00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:22.049
That flicker in House B, the night A went
dark? B died first. C claims: C, nine,

177
00:14:22.049 --> 00:14:27.261
from seven, and recovers seven, because
eight never opened and acknowledged nothing.

178
00:14:27.811 --> 00:14:35.322
Rewind. Had B opened, the Plate would read B,
eight, from eight, and C would recover eight.

179
00:14:38.175 --> 00:14:42.622
Inside the lantern, SQLite
keeps the room in a file.

180
00:14:42.622 --> 00:14:45.105
But it does not write changes there first.

181
00:14:45.455 --> 00:14:51.630
It writes them to a second file beside it:
a write-ahead log. Think of it as a ribbon.

182
00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.574
It starts with a leader,
thirty-two bytes long.

183
00:14:55.574 --> 00:15:00.113
Then frames: each a
twenty-four-byte tab and one page.

184
00:15:00.463 --> 00:15:04.480
On a transaction's last frame,
the tab records how many pages

185
00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:09.413
the database has after the commit.
Other frames hold zero there.

186
00:15:09.863 --> 00:15:13.926
We draw it as a red stamp.
That number is the commit mark.

187
00:15:16.183 --> 00:15:21.106
workerd's keeper hears every write
to the ribbon, holds the frames

188
00:15:21.106 --> 00:15:25.516
since the last stamp, and cuts only
when a stamped frame is complete.

189
00:15:25.866 --> 00:15:31.043
One cut, one whole transaction. Nothing
half-finished ever leaves the room.

190
00:15:31.493 --> 00:15:35.696
A transaction that rolls back
leaves frames with no stamp,

191
00:15:35.696 --> 00:15:37.564
and the next writes straight over them.

192
00:15:37.914 --> 00:15:41.594
When SQLite copies the
ribbon into its main file

193
00:15:41.594 --> 00:15:44.775
and starts a fresh one, the
keeper starts fresh too.

194
00:15:46.642 --> 00:15:50.137
The cut lengths go down
to the Well as scrolls:

195
00:15:50.137 --> 00:15:53.003
each page once, in its newest version.

196
00:15:53.353 --> 00:15:57.138
Every scroll's cord records
a checksum and its length,

197
00:15:57.138 --> 00:16:02.083
so a damaged or short scroll refuses
to be read, rather than read wrong.

198
00:16:02.533 --> 00:16:08.140
Each epoch's shelf starts with a
Book, a snapshot of what it inherited;

199
00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:11.773
a new Book goes down before
old scrolls are cleared.

200
00:16:12.223 --> 00:16:18.143
This is workerd's own format. celld
writes a different one, called LTX.

201
00:16:20.217 --> 00:16:23.073
Now the tick itself, in workerd.

202
00:16:23.073 --> 00:16:27.252
Message forty-two is written;
its reply waits behind the latch.

203
00:16:27.602 --> 00:16:31.769
The keeper cuts the transaction, and
sends the scroll down to the Well.

204
00:16:32.019 --> 00:16:36.757
Then, before it rings the bell, the keeper
looks up at the Plate one more time.

205
00:16:36.757 --> 00:16:39.426
Still A. Still epoch seven.

206
00:16:39.876 --> 00:16:43.892
Only then the bell. The
latch lifts. The tick.

207
00:16:44.542 --> 00:16:49.407
Finishing inside the room is not the
tick. The Well and the second look are.

208
00:16:49.407 --> 00:16:53.864
celld's gate holds even more:
errors, and every chunk of a stream.

209
00:16:56.142 --> 00:16:59.149
Pull the pin, and the
keeper never looks up.

210
00:16:59.599 --> 00:17:03.894
A sends its scroll with its lease in
order, then the whole house freezes,

211
00:17:03.894 --> 00:17:10.372
like the sleeper. B claims epoch eight, and
A's scroll lands on epoch seven's shelf.

212
00:17:10.722 --> 00:17:15.424
A rings the bell anyway: a tick
for a write that B may never see.

213
00:17:16.074 --> 00:17:19.951
Pin back. A looks up:
the Plate says eight.

214
00:17:19.951 --> 00:17:24.711
No bell; the letter becomes an error,
and the old scroll is cleared later.

215
00:17:25.261 --> 00:17:29.266
One honest limit: if a reply
is lost on its way home,

216
00:17:29.266 --> 00:17:31.738
the sender cannot tell
whether the write happened.

217
00:17:32.088 --> 00:17:38.519
A missing tick does not prove a write absent.
Durability is not the same as exactly once.

218
00:17:41.375 --> 00:17:48.061
Back to workerd's town, the night A went dark.
House C holds the Plate: nine, from seven.

219
00:17:48.511 --> 00:17:51.889
First it throws away its
own copy of the room.

220
00:17:52.189 --> 00:17:58.355
It reads the newest Book, then each scroll
in order, skipping pages already laid down.

221
00:17:58.655 --> 00:18:01.870
A missing scroll in the
middle stops the restore cold.

222
00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:06.297
A checksum can say a scroll is
damaged. Never what it said.

223
00:18:06.597 --> 00:18:09.952
Pages are laid down
again; no code is re-run.

224
00:18:10.252 --> 00:18:16.623
Message forty-two is back. Only then does C
send down its own Book, and open the Plate.

225
00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:20.933
That is the price. In workerd's cluster,

226
00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:24.577
every tick waits for the Well,
and a second look at the Plate.

227
00:18:24.877 --> 00:18:29.485
Our bar is illustrative: a sliver
of local work, then the long walk.

228
00:18:29.785 --> 00:18:34.892
Cloudflare's hosted relay, back in
2024, answered that with followers.

229
00:18:35.192 --> 00:18:40.369
celld uses followers too: a neighbour's
disk can answer much sooner than the Well.

230
00:18:40.819 --> 00:18:42.014
Change the lamps.

231
00:18:44.692 --> 00:18:47.037
This is celld's town, version 0.6.3.

232
00:18:47.037 --> 00:18:53.422
By default, the owner hands each
write to one or two neighbours.

233
00:18:53.872 --> 00:19:00.762
Each must press the page onto its own disk
before it answers. That press is called fsync.

234
00:19:01.062 --> 00:19:03.512
When every neighbour in
the proof has pressed,

235
00:19:03.512 --> 00:19:08.271
the bell can ring before the page
reaches the Well. The upload follows.

236
00:19:09.725 --> 00:19:12.673
Now count. The owner never counts itself.

237
00:19:13.123 --> 00:19:15.700
One house alone gets no fleet.

238
00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:19.635
It falls back on the Well's
proof: much slower, still correct.

239
00:19:19.935 --> 00:19:24.205
Two running houses: one
neighbour, enough for a proof.

240
00:19:24.205 --> 00:19:27.247
Three or more: two
neighbours, three copies.

241
00:19:27.547 --> 00:19:30.693
Until a second neighbour
joins, a fresh write's proof

242
00:19:30.693 --> 00:19:34.337
rests on two copies: the
owner's and one neighbour's.

243
00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:40.042
Now replay that night in celld's town:

244
00:19:40.042 --> 00:19:43.988
the owner dies while forty-two lives
only in the neighbours' notebooks.

245
00:19:44.288 --> 00:19:45.228
Pull the pin.

246
00:19:45.978 --> 00:19:49.925
In celld's Well, each epoch
writes under its own shelf,

247
00:19:49.925 --> 00:19:54.696
so a stale owner can only add to an
old shelf, never to the new owner's.

248
00:19:54.996 --> 00:20:02.256
That fences the Well. It cannot fetch what
only the neighbours hold. Forty-two is gone.

249
00:20:02.556 --> 00:20:06.955
Or the old owner was only asleep,
and wakes to ring one more bell.

250
00:20:07.605 --> 00:20:11.216
Pin back. Before its first
tick from the neighbours,

251
00:20:11.216 --> 00:20:15.069
each celld session creates its
own log record in the Well.

252
00:20:17.158 --> 00:20:20.942
So a successor reads that
record before it reads the Well.

253
00:20:21.392 --> 00:20:25.721
Absent: the old session never
ticked ahead of the Well.

254
00:20:25.721 --> 00:20:28.158
Sealed: someone already recovered it.

255
00:20:28.458 --> 00:20:35.060
Open, or still recovering: fence it with
a compare and swap. Seal every neighbour

256
00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:40.261
it can reach, collect their pages,
upload them, and mark the record sealed.

257
00:20:40.561 --> 00:20:46.713
Only then restore. With its neighbours
sealed, the old owner cannot win another bell.

258
00:20:49.383 --> 00:20:52.216
Now the hard rule. At
least one current neighbour

259
00:20:52.216 --> 00:20:55.350
must hand over its complete
range of retained pages.

260
00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:59.885
Two made-up cases. One
notebook is water-damaged,

261
00:20:59.885 --> 00:21:03.146
but the other is complete:
recovery proceeds.

262
00:21:03.796 --> 00:21:07.848
Now one notebook is incomplete,
and the other damaged.

263
00:21:07.848 --> 00:21:11.656
Nobody can prove the whole
tail, so the door stays shut.

264
00:21:11.956 --> 00:21:16.564
And a neighbour that answers with an
error has not proved its pages absent.

265
00:21:17.114 --> 00:21:21.235
The room would rather stay shut
than come back missing a promise.

266
00:21:23.508 --> 00:21:28.825
Houses that restart together still lend
each other their notebooks. Recovery can

267
00:21:28.825 --> 00:21:34.200
take minutes; a waiting request gives up
only after two hundred and forty tries.

268
00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:40.984
In our harbour the notebooks are whole. The
lantern relights; forty-two keeps its tick.

269
00:21:44.242 --> 00:21:48.393
Four pins, in the order
we pulled them: the Tooth,

270
00:21:48.393 --> 00:21:52.630
the band on the candle, the
second look, the neighbours' seal.

271
00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:55.390
Pull any one, and a promise breaks.

272
00:21:55.940 --> 00:22:00.061
So what travels, when a room
travels, is not the bucket alone.

273
00:22:00.361 --> 00:22:06.189
It is the bucket's refusals on one key,
and its fresh reads; leases, with clocks

274
00:22:06.189 --> 00:22:12.481
kept close; and, as celld asks, a supervisor
to restart a house that fenced itself.

275
00:22:12.781 --> 00:22:17.195
And a runtime that cuts the
ribbon, uploads and looks again;

276
00:22:17.195 --> 00:22:22.314
in celld's fleet, one that also
seals and collects before it replays.

277
00:22:23.975 --> 00:22:28.746
The small print, as of the
thirty-first of October, 2026.

278
00:22:29.196 --> 00:22:33.502
workerd's clustering is three
days old, and still experimental.

279
00:22:33.802 --> 00:22:39.026
Its tests check one writer,
forwarding, and takeover after a kill.

280
00:22:39.026 --> 00:22:41.499
We read them. We did not run them.

281
00:22:41.799 --> 00:22:45.154
The alarm tests check
the cases they stage.

282
00:22:45.154 --> 00:22:49.170
They do not establish exactly-once
effects for every application.

283
00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.757
celld documents its own limits,

284
00:22:51.757 --> 00:22:55.588
and wants bucket lifecycle rules
that leave its records alone.

285
00:22:55.888 --> 00:23:00.531
And workerd, on its own, is not
a sandbox for untrusted code.

286
00:23:03.008 --> 00:23:06.770
The two lamps, once more.
The announced plan stands,

287
00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:08.917
and the commit called itself a beginning.

288
00:23:09.267 --> 00:23:13.249
celld's code and ideas are
to be merged into workerd,

289
00:23:13.249 --> 00:23:18.159
and Ryan Dahl writes that the team's
future work goes into this shared platform.

290
00:23:18.809 --> 00:23:24.048
And the Well? It does not run your
code, make two rooms commit as one,

291
00:23:24.048 --> 00:23:28.531
promise exactly once, or keep
the town open when it goes quiet.

292
00:23:31.017 --> 00:23:34.604
Dawn over the harbour. The
lantern burns in House C,

293
00:23:34.604 --> 00:23:37.181
and the sender's slip
still carries its tick.

294
00:23:37.931 --> 00:23:41.286
A tick is not a feeling. It is a proof.

295
00:23:42.036 --> 00:23:47.443
The bytes were in the Well, and the Plate
still named its owner when the bell rang.

296
00:23:48.293 --> 00:23:51.508
The harbour was made up. The code is real.
